Subject: [PATCH] crosvm: allow bounded device process teardown and log slow children

The Linux cleanup path polls children for only 100 * 10 ms before killing
its entire process group, including the VMM. Successful guest shutdown
can therefore become SIGKILL when a child takes more than one second to
exit; the old cleanup comment incorrectly said 100 ms.

Allow a ten-second monotonic grace period. This gives multi-second
teardown time to finish while retaining a finite shutdown bound and the
existing group SIGKILL fallback. Do not delay children that exit promptly.
At one second, log one best-effort snapshot of remaining child PIDs,
comm and wchan to identify the slow process on the next occurrence.
Enumerate direct children by PPid in /proc/PID/stat so this also works
without CONFIG_PROC_CHILDREN. No particular device or flush operation
is assumed to be responsible.

Based on src/sys/linux/main.rs in crosvm
96e84c3f680e34e82e932ef911ae97d734121758. This generic Linux cleanup
change is suitable for proposing upstream; no shutdown ordering,
device reset or child exit-status semantics are changed.

--- a/src/sys/linux/main.rs
+++ b/src/sys/linux/main.rs
@@ -4,6 +4,7 @@
 
 use std::thread::sleep;
 use std::time::Duration;
+use std::time::Instant;
 
 use anyhow::anyhow;
 use anyhow::Context;
@@ -35,12 +36,56 @@
